\.dm — Dimension Modeling Language

\Purpose

\.dm describes objects, spaces, dimensions, geometry, fields, transformations, observations, and projections in arbitrary-dimensional spaces.

\The fundamental pipeline is:

\boxed{
\text{Define}
\rightarrow
\text{Model}
\rightarrow
\text{Transform}
\rightarrow
\text{Observe}
\rightarrow
\text{Project}
\rightarrow
\text{Render}
}

⸻

1. Minimal .dm file

dm 0.1
model Hypersphere {
    dimension 4
    axes [x, y, z, w]
    geometry {
        equation: x² + y² + z² + w² <= R²
        radius: R
    }
    observe {
        dimension: 3
        method: slice
        axis: w
    }
}

This is the smallest useful representation of our 4D hypersphere.

⸻

2. Dimensions

A dimension has a name, type, domain, and optionally a metric.

dimension 4 {
    axis x {
        type: spatial
        domain: real
    }
    axis y {
        type: spatial
        domain: real
    }
    axis z {
        type: spatial
        domain: real
    }
    axis w {
        type: spatial
        domain: real
    }
}

Supported dimension types could include:

spatial
temporal
compact
configuration
semantic
latent
relational
abstract

This is important because .dm shouldn’t automatically equate mathematical dimension with physical dimension.

⸻

3. Coordinates

Coordinates can be declared explicitly:

coordinates {
    x: real
    y: real
    z: real
    w: real
}

Or vector notation:

X=(x,y,z,w)

In .dm:

vector X = [x, y, z, w]

⸻

4. Geometry

Geometry defines the mathematical object.

Our hypersphere:

geometry {
    equation:
        x² + y² + z² + w² <= R²
    center:
        [0, 0, 0, 0]
    radius:
        R
}

We could also define parametric geometry:

geometry parametric {
    x = R sin(a) sin(b) cos(c)
    y = R sin(a) sin(b) sin(c)
    z = R sin(a) cos(b)
    w = R cos(a)
}

⸻

5. Topology

Geometry and topology should be separate.

topology {
    manifold: S3
    boundary: none
    connected: true
}

This allows .dm to represent objects whose important properties aren’t purely geometric.

⸻

6. Fields

A field assigns information to every point.

For example:

field density {
    type: scalar
    equation:
        density(x,y,z,w) =
            if x²+y²+z²+w² <= R²
            then 1
            else 0
}

Vector fields:

field velocity {
    type: vector
    components:
        [vx, vy, vz, vw]
}

Tensor fields:

field metric {
    type: tensor
    rank: 2
    components: g[μ,ν]
}

This gives .dm the ability to represent physics rather than merely shapes.

⸻

7. Transformations

A transformation changes the representation.

Translation:

transform move {
    vector: [a,b,c,d]
}

Rotation in the x-w plane:

\begin{aligned}
x' &= x\cos\theta-w\sin\theta\\
w' &= x\sin\theta+w\cos\theta
\end{aligned}

.dm:

transform rotate {
    plane: [x,w]
    angle: θ
}

This is particularly useful because a 4D object can be rotated in planes that don’t exist in ordinary 3D visualization.

⸻

8. Observation

This is one of the most important parts.

The object may exist in N dimensions, but the observer has access to only M.

observe {
    source: 4D
    target: 3D
    method: slice
    axis: w
    value: 0
}

Mathematically:

P_w(X)=
(x,y,z)

at:

w=w_0

⸻

9. Projection

A projection doesn’t necessarily remove a dimension—it can map it into another representation.

For example:

projection perspective4D {
    source: [x,y,z,w]
    target: [X,Y,Z]
    distance: d
}

One possible mathematical projection is:

X=\frac{dx}{d-w}

Y=\frac{dy}{d-w}

Z=\frac{dz}{d-w}

Now w influences apparent scale and position.

That could produce a much more intuitive visualization of 4D geometry.

⸻

10. Time

A .dm model can evolve:

time {
    variable: t
    start: 0
    end: 10
    step: 0.01
}

Then geometry can depend on time:

radius:
    R(t) = R0 + v*t

So the object becomes:

\mathcal O(x,y,z,w,t)

⸻

11. Compactification

Now we can directly represent the physics we’ve been discussing.

compactification {
    axis: w
    topology: S1
    radius: R
    identification:
        w ~ w + 2πR
}

Mathematically:

w\sim w+2\pi R

The .dm engine now knows that w is periodic.

⸻

12. Quantum modes

We could even describe Kaluza–Klein modes:

modes {
    axis: w
    momentum:
        p[n] = nħ/R
    mass:
        m[n]² = m0² + n²ħ²/(R²c²)
}

So the same .dm model can describe both:

geometry

and

physical consequences of that geometry.

⸻

13. Relational dimensions

Here’s where .dm goes beyond traditional geometry.

relation network {
    node A
    node B
    node C
    edge A -> B
    edge B -> C
    edge C -> A
}

There is no requirement that A, B and C have physical coordinates.

The structure itself is the object.

⸻

14. Semantic dimensions

We can represent abstract dimensions too:

dimension confidence {
    type: semantic
    domain: [0,1]
}

An object might therefore have:

dimensions {
    spatial: [x,y,z]
    temporal: [t]
    semantic: [confidence, relevance]
    latent: [l1,l2,...,l1024]
}

That becomes extremely useful for AI.

⸻

15. LMLM integration

I’d make LMLM the intelligence layer, while .dm becomes the world/model layer.

             LMLM
              │
       understanding/reasoning
              │
              ▼
           .dm MODEL
              │
     ┌────────┼────────┐
     ▼        ▼        ▼
  Geometry  Physics  Semantics
     │        │        │
     └────────┼────────┘
              ▼
         N-D ENGINE
              │
       ┌──────┼──────┐
       ▼      ▼      ▼
      3D     2D     VR

LMLM could ask:

load Hypersphere.dm
observe axis=w value=0
rotate plane=[x,w] angle=45°
project target=3D
render

And the engine produces the corresponding visualization.

⸻

16. .dm execution model

The interpreter could use:

\boxed{
Parser
\rightarrow
AST
\rightarrow
Dimension Graph
\rightarrow
Geometry Engine
\rightarrow
Projection Engine
\rightarrow
Renderer
}

The AST might look conceptually like:

Model
├── Dimensions
│   ├── x
│   ├── y
│   ├── z
│   └── w
├── Geometry
│   └── Hypersphere
├── Fields
├── Transformations
├── Observation
├── Projection
└── Rendering

⸻

17. The .dm ecosystem

I’d define these initial file types:

.dm       Dimension Model
.dmx      Dimension Module
.dms      Dimension Scene
.dmf      Dimension Field
.dmp      Dimension Projection
.dmt      Dimension Transform

But .dm should remain the primary format. The others can come later if there’s a real need.

⸻

18. The first real test

Our first canonical test file should be:

dm 0.1
model Hypersphere {
    dimension 4
    axes [x,y,z,w]
    geometry {
        equation:
            x² + y² + z² + w² <= R²
    }
    topology {
        manifold: S3
        boundary: none
    }
    observation {
        target: 3D
        method: slice
        axis: w
        range: [-R,R]
    }
    projection {
        method: perspective
    }
    animation {
        parameter: w
        steps: 120
    }
    render {
        mode: volume
        interactive: true
    }
}



